# Copy to `.env.local` for local dev. In production these are set in the
# Vercel dashboard under Settings > Environment Variables — NOT in this file
# and NOT committed. `.env.local` is gitignored; this template is not.
#
# The inquiry route returns HTTP 503 rather than a false success when SMTP is
# missing, so a misconfigured deploy surfaces immediately instead of silently
# discarding leads.

# --- SMTP: required for the inquiry form to deliver mail ---
# Gmail: SMTP_PASS must be a 16-character App Password, not the account
# password. Generate at https://myaccount.google.com/apppasswords
# Rotate it if it is ever pasted into a chat, ticket, or screenshot.
#
# EMAIL ROUTING
#
# `SMTP_USER` / `CONTACT_EMAIL` is the inbox that receives all submissions.
# It is also the Gmail account we authenticate as, because Gmail SMTP
# requires the From address to match the authenticated account (or an
# approved "Send mail as" alias).
#
# The brand email displayed on the site is `benzartdecor@gmail.com`. If you
# want that address to appear in the From header, add it as a "Send mail as"
# alias in the receiving account, then set SMTP_FROM accordingly.
SMTP_HOST=smtp.gmail.com
SMTP_PORT=587
SMTP_USER=
SMTP_PASS=

# Must match the authenticated Gmail account unless benzartdecor@gmail.com
# is configured as a verified alias in the receiving account.
SMTP_FROM="Benzart Resin" <you@example.com>

# Inbox that receives inquiries and newsletter sign-ups.
CONTACT_EMAIL=

# --- Local dev: "self-signed certificate in certificate chain" ---
# CONFIRMED on this project's dev machine, and the cause of a full outage of
# both forms. AVG Antivirus ("Mail Shield") intercepts the connection to
# smtp.gmail.com and re-signs it with "AVG Web/Mail Shield Root". That root is
# installed in the Windows certificate store, so browsers and most tools accept
# it, but Node ships its own CA list (~120 roots) and does not read the OS
# store by default — so Node alone rejects the chain. The forms return HTTP 502
# while the credentials are perfectly valid.
#
# The tell: the same credentials authenticate fine from a plain `node` script
# run with NODE_USE_SYSTEM_CA=1 in the same shell, while the dev server fails.
#
# Fix (persisted for your Windows user, survives reboots and new terminals):
#   [Environment]::SetEnvironmentVariable('NODE_USE_SYSTEM_CA','1','User')
# Or per session:
#   $env:NODE_USE_SYSTEM_CA=1; npm run dev
#
# Do NOT "fix" this with NODE_TLS_REJECT_UNAUTHORIZED=0 or
# `tls: { rejectUnauthorized: false }`. That disables certificate verification
# for the whole process and would silently accept a genuine attacker.
#
# This is a local-machine artefact. It does not occur on Vercel, whose Node
# processes see Gmail's real certificate chain.

# --- Site origin ---
# Drives canonicals, hreflang, sitemap, robots, and llms.txt.
# Leave UNSET on Vercel production so the code default is used. Set it on
# preview deployments so previews emit their own canonicals instead of
# claiming to be production (which would tell Google prod is a duplicate).
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=https://benzartresin.com

# --- Analytics ---
# Set to an empty string on preview deploys so staging traffic never pollutes
# production GA data.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_GA_ID=G-G78WJFE4P2
